5625, -0.0012578964233398438, -0.02044677734375, -0.0008907318115234375, -0.028961181640625, -0.026824951171875, 0.0207366943359375, -0.005924224853515625, -0.00482940673828125, -0.01076507568359375, 0.0159912109375, 0.01342010498046875, 0.005706787109375, -0.047607421875, -0.0024585723876953125, -0.0181732177734375, 0.0009765625, 0.0029964447021484375, 0.007686614990234375, 0.01041412353515625, -0.0189361572265625, 0.00830078125, -0.006122589111328125, -0.015655517578125, 0.00010484457015991211, -0.0226898193359375, -0.04461669921875, 0.0032958984375, 0.01395416259765625, 0.008819580078125, 0.01525115966796875, 0.00782012939453125, -0.01265716552734375, 0.0038967132568359375, -0.01617431640625, -0.01654052734375, -0.03070068359375, 0.0103759765625, 0.01134490966796875, -0.0312042236328125, 0.007419586181640625, 0.01910400390625, -0.007801055908203125, 0.0235595703125, -0.006061553955078125, -0.006404876708984375, 0.015167236328125, 0.0027790069580078125, 0.029937744140625, 0.0017070770263671875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "The emergence of organized spatiotemporal patterns is a hallmark of oscillatory systems, from neural populations to engineered networks. However, reliably identifying and tracking these patterns\u2014especially during transient dynamics\u2014remains a significant challenge. Existing methods often focus on global synchronization or amplitude-based measures, which may overlook important local phase relationships. To address this gap, we introduce a framework based on spatial ordinal patterns that captures the local spatial ordering of oscillator phases. Specifically, for each oscillator, we compare its phase to those of its neighbors, encoding the relative ordering as a symbolic pattern; when phases are nearly equal, we introduce a dedicated symbol to represent ties, ensuring robust characterization of both gradients and clusters. Focusing on phase rather than amplitude allows us to directly probe synchronization and wave propagation phenomena, which are central to many oscillatory systems.\n\nFrom these symbolic representations, we define a spatial permutation entropy by computing the diversity of observed ordinal patterns across the network at each time point. This entropy quantifies the complexity of the spatiotemporal organization, enabling the detection of transient events and regime transitions. We validate our approach on synthetic oscillator networks, demonstrating that it distinguishes between phase-locked states with identical global synchronization but different spatial arrangements, as well as characterizing partially synchronized and desynchronized regimes. For example, in networks exhibiting chimera states, our method reveals distinct spatial patterning not captured by global order parameters. We further apply the framework to resting-state EEG recordings from human volunteers, where it differentiates between eyes-open and eyes-closed conditions within individuals\u2014outperforming traditional amplitude-based entropy measures in sensitivity to spatial phase structure. Our results establish spatial ordinal pattern analysis as a powerful tool for uncovering and quantifying the rich spatiotemporal dynamics of oscillatory systems."}}